<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Manipur</h1><p class="st-meta">33 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Imphal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Meitei kingdom of the Imphal valley, one of the longest-lived monarchies of South Asia, which kept its own royal chronicle for nearly two thousand years of claimed history.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The royal chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheitharol-kumbaba">Cheitharol Kumbaba</a>, traditionally dates the founding to 33 CE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nongda-lairen-pakhangba">Nongda Lairen Pakhangba</a> became ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningthouja-dynasty">Ningthouja</a> clan. Over the following centuries the Ningthouja kings absorbed the other Meitei clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a> valley into a single kingdom known to its people as Kangleipak.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through the lallup system, under which adult men owed periodic labour and military service to the state. In the early 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamheiba">Pamheiba</a>, known as Garib Niwaz, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> the court religion, though the older Sanamahi faith survived. Manipuri cavalry and polo were famous, and the kingdom fought repeated wars with the Burmese kingdoms to the east.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Burmese occupation from 1819 was ended in 1826 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gambhir-singh">Gambhir Singh</a> with British help. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-manipur-war">Anglo-Manipur War</a> of 1891 Manipur became a princely state under British control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhachandra-singh">Bodhachandra Singh</a> signed a merger agreement with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a> in 1949, and Manipur became a full state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India</a> in 1972.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-manipur.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Manipur" width="320" height="258"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengal_gazetteer_1907-9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>33 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Imphal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pamheiba (Garib Niwaz), Gambhir Singh, Bodhachandra Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vaishnavism, Sanamahism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Meitei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Manipur, Kangleipak</dd></dl></aside></div>
